\section*{Source A}

"The town has three hundred houses, a weekly fair, and a ferry of four
boats. Grain from seven villages crosses here on its way to the coast."

- from a district gazetteer of 1874, describing a river-bend market town

\section*{Do you know?}

Step-wells were built where rivers ran dry in summer. Their long staircases
let people reach water however far it fell. Some step-wells are more than
eight hundred years old and are still in use.

\section*{8.2 When the River Floods}

A river that gives can also take. Flood water spoils stored grain and cuts
villages off from one another. Communities answered with raised granaries,
earthen embankments, and rules about who repairs which stretch of the bank.

